  user_input {
    user_response: "# STARFALL \342\200\224 Source-Based Flight Control Restoration Using NEON ORBIT\n\n## Objective\n\nThe current STARFALL mouse flight controls still feel unsatisfactory after multiple rounds of sensitivity and virtual joystick adjustments.\n\nStop inventing new control formulas.\n\nI found a closely related Three.js space fighter project:\n\nhttps://github.com/ATLBitLab/neon-orbit\n\nIts documented mouse control architecture is particularly relevant:\n\n- Mouse acts as a virtual stick, not an FPS look control.\n- Relative mouse movement produces virtual stick deflection.\n- The stick self-centers.\n- Ship turn rate determines how quickly the ship rotates.\n- Sensitivity and self-centering parameters are centralized in `src/core/input.ts`.\n\nI want you to inspect this actual implementation and use it as the primary technical reference for STARFALL.\n\n## 1. Inspect the Reference Repository\n\nRead the following parts of NEON ORBIT:\n\n- `src/core/input.ts`\n- The ship flight model.\n- Ship turn-rate specifications.\n- Input-to-controls mapping.\n- Relevant simulation tests.\n- Any other files required to understand the complete mouse-to-ship-rotation pipeline.\n\nTrace the actual implementation.\n\nIdentify:\n\n1. How relative mouse movement changes stick deflection.\n2. How stick self-centering works.\n3. How sensitivity is applied.\n4. How maximum deflection is limited.\n5. How deflection becomes ship angular velocity.\n6. How angular acceleration and damping work.\n7. Which values control the final flight feel.\n\nDo not guess these details from the README. Read the source code.\n\n## 2. Check Licensing Before Copying Code\n\nInspect the repository\'s LICENSE file and relevant third-party notices.\n\nIf the license permits reuse:\n\n- Reuse or adapt the relevant input-control implementation.\n- Preserve required copyright and license notices.\n- Document which portions were adapted.\n\nIf no suitable license is present or reuse is not permitted:\n\n- Do not copy the source code.\n- Use it as a technical reference only.\n- Implement an independently written equivalent based on the documented behavior.\n\nReport the licensing result before claiming code reuse.\n\n## 3. Compare Against STARFALL\n\nInspect STARFALL\'s current implementation.\n\nCompare both projects:\n\n- Input collection.\n- Virtual stick state.\n- Mouse sensitivity.\n- Self-centering.\n- Deadzone.\n- Maximum deflection.\n- Turn-rate mapping.\n- Angular acceleration.\n- Angular damping.\n- Camera follow.\n\nIdentify exactly why STARFALL feels worse.\n\nPay special attention to duplicated sensitivity multipliers, excessive smoothing, incorrect input accumulation, and conflicting camera or flight-controller updates.\n\nDo not immediately replace the entire flight system.\n\n## 4. Adopt the Reference Control Architecture\n\nUse NEON ORBIT\'s virtual-stick architecture as the primary reference.\n\nThe intended control behavior:\n\n- Mouse relative movement pushes a virtual stick.\n- Stick deflection determines the requested steering direction and magnitude.\n- The stick smoothly returns toward center when the mouse stops moving.\n- Ship turn rate controls actual rotation speed.\n- Maximum stick deflection is bounded.\n- The ship continues turning while the stick remains deflected.\n- Returning the stick toward center smoothly reduces turning.\n\nKeep mouse input and flight physics separate.\n\nDo not implement an FPS-style camera look system.\n\nDo not stack additional sensitivity curves on top of the reference architecture without a demonstrated reason.\n\n## 5. Preserve STARFALL Requirements\n\n- Preserve the successfully fixed Pointer Lock implementation.\n- Continue using `movementX` and `movementY`.\n- Never introduce cursor recentering.\n- Physical screen edges must never interrupt input.\n- Keep third-person chase camera.\n- Keep arcade 6-DoF flight.\n- Preserve boost behavior.\n- Preserve existing combat and mission systems.\n\nThe current 30% \303\227 35% rounded-rectangle aim boundary is provisional. Inspect whether the reference architecture requires a different logical stick range. Do not confuse a visual reticle boundary with the virtual stick\'s internal deflection limit.\n\n## 6. Calibration\n\nDo not invent arbitrary sensitivity values.\n\nExtract the actual relevant parameters from the reference implementation.\n\nUse those values as a starting point where compatible.\n\nIf STARFALL\'s coordinate system or flight model differs, explain the necessary conversions.\n\nKeep the following values centralized and configurable:\n\n- Mouse sensitivity.\n- Stick self-centering speed.\n- Deadzone.\n- Maximum stick deflection.\n- Yaw turn rate.\n- Pitch turn rate.\n- Angular acceleration.\n- Angular damping.\n\nTune only after the architecture is correctly integrated.\n\n## 7. Browser Testing\n\nTest the actual STARFALL browser game.\n\nVerify:\n\n1. Small mouse movements produce precise steering.\n2. Stopping mouse movement allows the virtual stick to self-center.\n3. Holding the stick deflected produces continuous turning.\n4. Maximum deflection does not produce uncontrollable rotation.\n5. Returning toward center smoothly reduces turning.\n6. Pointer Lock remains functional at physical screen edges.\n7. Flight remains responsive.\n8. Camera follow remains smooth.\n9. Boost behavior remains intact.\n10. Different frame rates produce consistent controls.\n\nIf possible, compare the reference project\'s live demo and STARFALL side by side.\n\n## 8. Scope\n\nFocus on mouse input, virtual stick behavior, flight response, and directly related settings.\n\nDo not redesign unrelated game systems.\n\nDo not remove the successful Pointer Lock fix.\n\nDo not claim that the result matches NEON ORBIT without actual comparison.\n\n## 9. Final Report\n\nReport:\n\n1. Reference files inspected.\n2. License findings.\n3. Actual reference input architecture.\n4. Root causes of STARFALL\'s poor handling.\n5. Which implementation details were reused or independently recreated.\n6. Final calibration values.\n7. Browser testing results.\n8. Remaining differences from the reference.\n\nPrioritize reproducing the reference control architecture faithfully over inventing another control system."
    items {
      text: "# STARFALL \342\200\224 Source-Based Flight Control Restoration Using NEON ORBIT\n\n## Objective\n\nThe current STARFALL mouse flight controls still feel unsatisfactory after multiple rounds of sensitivity and virtual joystick adjustments.\n\nStop inventing new control formulas.\n\nI found a closely related Three.js space fighter project:\n\nhttps://github.com/ATLBitLab/neon-orbit\n\nIts documented mouse control architecture is particularly relevant:\n\n- Mouse acts as a virtual stick, not an FPS look control.\n- Relative mouse movement produces virtual stick deflection.\n- The stick self-centers.\n- Ship turn rate determines how quickly the ship rotates.\n- Sensitivity and self-centering parameters are centralized in `src/core/input.ts`.\n\nI want you to inspect this actual implementation and use it as the primary technical reference for STARFALL.\n\n## 1. Inspect the Reference Repository\n\nRead the following parts of NEON ORBIT:\n\n- `src/core/input.ts`\n- The ship flight model.\n- Ship turn-rate specifications.\n- Input-to-controls mapping.\n- Relevant simulation tests.\n- Any other files required to understand the complete mouse-to-ship-rotation pipeline.\n\nTrace the actual implementation.\n\nIdentify:\n\n1. How relative mouse movement changes stick deflection.\n2. How stick self-centering works.\n3. How sensitivity is applied.\n4. How maximum deflection is limited.\n5. How deflection becomes ship angular velocity.\n6. How angular acceleration and damping work.\n7. Which values control the final flight feel.\n\nDo not guess these details from the README. Read the source code.\n\n## 2. Check Licensing Before Copying Code\n\nInspect the repository\'s LICENSE file and relevant third-party notices.\n\nIf the license permits reuse:\n\n- Reuse or adapt the relevant input-control implementation.\n- Preserve required copyright and license notices.\n- Document which portions were adapted.\n\nIf no suitable license is present or reuse is not permitted:\n\n- Do not copy the source code.\n- Use it as a technical reference only.\n- Implement an independently written equivalent based on the documented behavior.\n\nReport the licensing result before claiming code reuse.\n\n## 3. Compare Against STARFALL\n\nInspect STARFALL\'s current implementation.\n\nCompare both projects:\n\n- Input collection.\n- Virtual stick state.\n- Mouse sensitivity.\n- Self-centering.\n- Deadzone.\n- Maximum deflection.\n- Turn-rate mapping.\n- Angular acceleration.\n- Angular damping.\n- Camera follow.\n\nIdentify exactly why STARFALL feels worse.\n\nPay special attention to duplicated sensitivity multipliers, excessive smoothing, incorrect input accumulation, and conflicting camera or flight-controller updates.\n\nDo not immediately replace the entire flight system.\n\n## 4. Adopt the Reference Control Architecture\n\nUse NEON ORBIT\'s virtual-stick architecture as the primary reference.\n\nThe intended control behavior:\n\n- Mouse relative movement pushes a virtual stick.\n- Stick deflection determines the requested steering direction and magnitude.\n- The stick smoothly returns toward center when the mouse stops moving.\n- Ship turn rate controls actual rotation speed.\n- Maximum stick deflection is bounded.\n- The ship continues turning while the stick remains deflected.\n- Returning the stick toward center smoothly reduces turning.\n\nKeep mouse input and flight physics separate.\n\nDo not implement an FPS-style camera look system.\n\nDo not stack additional sensitivity curves on top of the reference architecture without a demonstrated reason.\n\n## 5. Preserve STARFALL Requirements\n\n- Preserve the successfully fixed Pointer Lock implementation.\n- Continue using `movementX` and `movementY`.\n- Never introduce cursor recentering.\n- Physical screen edges must never interrupt input.\n- Keep third-person chase camera.\n- Keep arcade 6-DoF flight.\n- Preserve boost behavior.\n- Preserve existing combat and mission systems.\n\nThe current 30% \303\227 35% rounded-rectangle aim boundary is provisional. Inspect whether the reference architecture requires a different logical stick range. Do not confuse a visual reticle boundary with the virtual stick\'s internal deflection limit.\n\n## 6. Calibration\n\nDo not invent arbitrary sensitivity values.\n\nExtract the actual relevant parameters from the reference implementation.\n\nUse those values as a starting point where compatible.\n\nIf STARFALL\'s coordinate system or flight model differs, explain the necessary conversions.\n\nKeep the following values centralized and configurable:\n\n- Mouse sensitivity.\n- Stick self-centering speed.\n- Deadzone.\n- Maximum stick deflection.\n- Yaw turn rate.\n- Pitch turn rate.\n- Angular acceleration.\n- Angular damping.\n\nTune only after the architecture is correctly integrated.\n\n## 7. Browser Testing\n\nTest the actual STARFALL browser game.\n\nVerify:\n\n1. Small mouse movements produce precise steering.\n2. Stopping mouse movement allows the virtual stick to self-center.\n3. Holding the stick deflected produces continuous turning.\n4. Maximum deflection does not produce uncontrollable rotation.\n5. Returning toward center smoothly reduces turning.\n6. Pointer Lock remains functional at physical screen edges.\n7. Flight remains responsive.\n8. Camera follow remains smooth.\n9. Boost behavior remains intact.\n10. Different frame rates produce consistent controls.\n\nIf possible, compare the reference project\'s live demo and STARFALL side by side.\n\n## 8. Scope\n\nFocus on mouse input, virtual stick behavior, flight response, and directly related settings.\n\nDo not redesign unrelated game systems.\n\nDo not remove the successful Pointer Lock fix.\n\nDo not claim that the result matches NEON ORBIT without actual comparison.\n\n## 9. Final Report\n\nReport:\n\n1. Reference files inspected.\n2. License findings.\n3. Actual reference input architecture.\n4. Root causes of STARFALL\'s poor handling.\n5. Which implementation details were reused or independently recreated.\n6. Final calibration values.\n7. Browser testing results.\n8. Remaining differences from the reference.\n\nPrioritize reproducing the reference control architecture faithfully over inventing another control system."
