    combined_output {
      full: "<truncated 24 bytes>\neen wrong twice \342\200\224 once with bolts left on tick boundaries while ships were smoothed, and oncewith the camera smoothed while the wreck it was locked onto was not. Smoothing one consumer of ashared pose relocates the mismatch rather than removing it, and a relative mismatch between twothings that should be pinned together reads worse than plain judder. `simcheck` asserts theinvariant directly by drawing one frozen simulation state at three blends and checking the middleis the midpoint of the outer two.**Gameplay randomness comes from the run seed, never `Math.random()`.** `Game.start` takes anoptional seed in its `MatchSetup` and reports it back through `snapshot().seed`. Everything that decides an outcome\342\200\224 squadron order, arrival points, AI wander and break timing, gun spread \342\200\224 draws from asubstream of it (`subRng` in `src/core/rng.ts`), so a run is a function of its seed and itsinputs and nothing else. Cosmetic scatter \342\200\224 particles, camera shake, wreck sparks \342\200\224 is exemptand still uses `Math.random()` directly.Two viewers of one run may see different sparks; they may not see different hulls. `simcheck`asserts this by playing the same seed twice and comparing, so a stray `Math.random()` on a paththat decides something fails the build rather than surfacing later as a desync.**Everything is procedural.** No textures, no models, no audio files. Hulls and stations arecoarse primitives merged into non-indexed geometry so recomputed normals stay hard-faceted;`src/core/geo.ts` is the single source of the *-Z is forward* convention. The planet is onedraw call \342\200\224 a faceted icosphere displaced and coloured per-facet from a sum-of-sine-bandselevation field. Sound is oscillators and filtered noise built on demand.**Additive glow stacks fast under bloom.** Ship accent materials sit around 0.45 opacity. Near1.0 a 30-unit fighter becomes a white plasma ball. The arena boundary grid needs a *dim colour*as well as low opacity, or bloom rediscovers it and paints a cage over the sky.**The patrol boundary vetoes outward thrust rather than pushing back.** `velocity` chasesnose \303\227 maxSpeed at rate `grip`, so the engine asserts the most acceleration exactly whenvelocity is zero \342\200\224 which means any additive counter-force has a depth where the two cancel andthe ship hangs motionless against the wall. Raising the force only relocates the stall. See thecomment in `Ship.integrate`.**A loss resolves before it finishes.** Taking the fatal hit does not report the run \342\200\224 it sealsthe scoreline and hands the next `DEATH_SEQUENCE` seconds to the wreck, which coasts and tumblesfor a beat before it goes up, then cooks off in the debris while the squadron flies on around it.The debrief comes up after. Two consequences worth knowing: the result is banked at the moment ofdeath, so a hostile burning up in the star during the animation cannot post a bounty to a pilotwho is already dead; and the game refuses to pause while `game.dying`, because a paused explosionwith a debrief queued behind it is a dead end. The tumble is applied to the ship\'s *visual* only \342\200\224the chase camera sits in the ship\'s own frame, so spinning the hull\'s transform would spin theshot instead. All of it is in `beginDeathSequence` / `stepDeathSequence` in `src/game/game.ts`.## Checks### Authoritative server`src/server/match.ts` runs the real arena and existing host protocol in Node,without a browser, renderer, DOM or audio device. Every seat, including P1, isremote or AI-driven. A connection must send a compatible HELLO before it gets aseat; the server fixes the hulls and seed. Received intents still pass throughthe existing seat binding, replay rejection and input admission. Only theserver\'s `step()` advances the match, by 1/60 second. Closing the match closesits channels and disposes its simulation and environment.`src/server/listener.ts` runs that match behind a WebSocket listener with rooms,packet and rate limits, backpressure, and lifecycle cleanup. The browser canjoin its rooms while the existing WebRTC host path remains available. Theruntime caps connections at eight per match and expires unseated handshakesafter 300 server ticks. The listener owns scheduling and room lifetime.The arena uses the same construction as the browser, including stations, minesand pods. It currently allocates three.js geometry even though it never renders;this keeps collision and pickup placement identical while establishing theboundary. Removing those allocations is a later measured optimization.Run a reproducible local load measurement:```bashnpm run bench:server -- 60 4 1  # former 60 Hz snapshotsnpm run bench:server -- 60 4 2  # dedicated server\'s 30 Hz snapshotsnpm run bench:server -- 60 4 2 --all-drone  # four Drone pilots at 30 Hznpm run bench:server -- 60 4 2 --all-hornet # four Hornet pilots at 30 Hznpm run check:bandwidth        # all three four-seat rosters checked```It prints startup and tick timings, CPU time, process RSS and encoded outboundbytes. The benchmark runs faster than real time and excludes sockets, TLS andconcurrent rooms, so it does not establish hosting capacity. The dedicatedlistener still simulates and accepts input at 60 Hz but sends complete snapshotsat 30 Hz; browser-hosted WebRTC keeps its existing cadence and protocol.All three rosters must stay under 160 KB/s per client (about 1.3 Mbps).Main\'s all-Hornet roster already used about 138 KB/s at 30 Hz, above the old130 KB/s gate that measured only the mixed roster; this explicit ceiling coversall three without lowering the snapshot cadence.`simcheck` exercises all-remote seating, P1 snapshot mirroring,forgery/replay, takeover, bounded handshakes and shutdown.### Existing checks```bashnpm run check          # typecheck + headless simulation + balancenpm run check:sim      # simulation only, ~1snpm run check:balance  # the balance matrix and its contract, ~4snpm run build          # typecheck + production bundle````scripts/simcheck.ts` runs the real flight model, projectiles, AI and game loop in Node \342\200\224 it isall pure maths over three.js vector types, no canvas needed. It exists because in-browserverification proved unreliable: a throttled tab stops firing `requestAnimationFrame`, whichsilently freezes the loop and makes every behavioural observation meaningless. It asserts thecombat contract (hits land, kills register, friendly fire is off), the hull quirks, theboundary, the power-up pods (placement, collection, respawn, that a boosted bolt still doesexactly its spec damage, that a Shield refuses damage without crediting the shooter, and thatboth timed buffs stack, expire and do not survive a respawn), the BFG (spool, abort, ammo,falloff, self-damage, mine chaining, that charging really does silence the guns on the firstframe after warp-in, that a long hold cannot spend the second round, and that a Shield doesnot make the blast free), that clearing the roster reports a win, and that a fatal hit playsits death animation out in full before the debrief takes the screen.`scripts/balance.ts` is the same idea pointed at fairness instead of correctness. It flies pinnedduels \342\200\224 every airframe against every other, every bolt on target \342\200\224 and prints alpha strike,burst and sustained DPS, a time-to-kill matrix, and what each hazard costs each hull. Those are*ceilings* with the flying removed, which is the only way to compare guns; the Wasp\'s actualdefence is that nobody gets to shoot it under laboratory conditions. It then asserts the designcontract: trigger discipline must out-damage mashing, the spec sheet must match what the gunsmeasure, no airframe may run away with the firepower ranking, no matchup may end in under 0.6s,and no hazard may one-shot a hull.In `npm run dev`, `window.__neon` exposes the current screen, a read-only run snapshot(including the bearing to the locked target\'s lead point) and `start(shipId)`. Dev builds only.## Deploying to VercelStatic output, framework auto-detected, `vercel.json` already committed:```bashnpx vercel          # previewnpx vercel --prod   # production```Or import the repo in the Vercel dashboard and accept the defaults \342\200\224 build `npm run build`,output `dist`. There is nothing server-side to configure."
    }
    command_line: "ls -la /tmp/neon-orbit && echo \"=== PACKAGE.JSON ===\" && cat /tmp/neon-orbit/package.json && echo \"=== LICENSE FILES ===\" && find /tmp/neon-orbit -iname \'*license*\' -o -iname \'*notice*\' -o -iname \'*copying*\' && echo \"=== README.MD ===\" && cat /tmp/neon-orbit/README.md"
