  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing the findings**\n\nI\'m incredibly excited! The inspection was crucial, and every detail is perfectly clear now, verified directly from the source code and the GitHub API. I\'m now turning to an extremely rigorous engineering analysis of everything we\'ve just uncovered. First on the agenda is a critical assessment of the license findings.\n\n\n**Confirming License Status**\n\nI\'ve confirmed no permissive open-source license applies to the main NEON ORBIT source code. The initial inspection revealed the absence of a license file, a `private` flag in `package.json`, and a `null` license field from the GitHub API. The lone license file, `public/audio/LICENSE.txt`, explicitly covers *only* the audio assets, so I must treat all source code as fully reserved. Therefore, I must adhere to the user\'s instructions and use the codebase as a technical reference only, independently writing STARFALL\'s equivalent.\n\n\n\n**Mapping Mouse to Control**\n\nOkay, here\'s the current state: I\'m dissecting how raw mouse input translates into ship control, focusing on the virtual stick abstraction. It\'s clear that the sensitivity is precisely defined (1/300 deflection per pixel). There\'s a simple, uncluttered clamp to prevent deflection from exceeding normalized limits. I\'m noting the absence of pixel scaling, rounded clamping, and deadzones. The axis mapping, (x for yaw, y for screen-down) is also clear. I\'m now looking at stick self-centering, and how this controls the camera.\n\n\n**Examining Stick Recentre**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into the stick recentering behavior. The code reveals a frame-rate-independent exponential decay using `Math.exp(-STICK_RECENTRE * dt)`. The half-life is around 0.433 seconds, causing the stick deflection to smoothly return to zero when the mouse stops moving, which feels incredibly natural in the game. I find this behavior critical for the ship\'s smooth and controlled turning. I\'m also confirming the axis sign convention.\n\n\n**Verifying Pitch Inversion**\n\nI\'m now deep in the weeds with STARFALL\'s coordinate system and pitch inversion. The investigation has confirmed the expected pitch-up direction. I had to verify that nose-up translates to increased Y-coordinate for the `_AXIS_X` rotation using right-handed Three.js. After reviewing the related lines of code, the findings are rock-solid, even in light of the `invertY` setting. So I should reverse the sign with the setting turned on and keep it positive if the setting is turned off. I\'ve noted how mouse up (`movementY < 0`) needs to map to a positive pitch (`mousePitch > 0`).\n\n\n**Confirming Yaw Behavior**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in the yaw behavior verification, and I\'ve validated the consistency in coordinate systems and pitch/yaw sign conventions between STARFALL and NEON ORBIT. I can now confirm that Three.js right-handed coordinates match the conventions for yaw rotation. Mouse-right translates to nose-left, and mouse-left translates to nose-right. The results are consistent with an intuitive feel. I\'m now certain of correct control mappings.\n\n\n**Verifying Yaw Rate**\n\nI\'m now comparing NEON ORBIT\'s yaw rate with STARFALL\'s corresponding behavior, and I have made an important discovery. I\'ve found a 1:1 match in coordinate and sign conventions. The NEON ORBIT code directly applies the stick deflection as a turn-rate command, without any added inertia. I\'m taking notes on how this translates to STARFALL.\n\n\n\n**Comparing Angular Rates**\n\nI\'m now comparing NEON ORBIT and STARFALL\'s angular rates, and they are quite similar. It turns out that STARFALL\'s values are already quite close to NEON ORBIT\'s for the interceptor, striker and destroyer. Specifically, I\'m focusing on how mouse input translates to ship rotation: the mouse input translates into `stickX/stickY` which decays exponentially, with a rate of 1.6/s. These values directly map to pitch, yaw and roll.\n\n\n**Examining Camera Behavior**\n\nI\'m now investigating NEON ORBIT\'s chase camera, specifically `POSITION_FOLLOW` at 7.5 and `ROTATION_FOLLOW` at 11. The camera is positioned behind and above the ship, using the ship\'s own frame, and looks straight ahead. It uses the ship\'s `_up` vector directly, using exponential lerp for position and slerp for rotation. I am also checking the HUD reticle, noting it projects the ship\'s gun line onto the screen, so the reticle shows where the nose is pointing, not a separate cursor box. I\'m noting these values for direct comparison to STARFALL.\n\n\n**Identifying Key Differences**\n\nI\'ve discovered a critical difference: STARFALL\'s joystick lacks self-centering, unlike NEON ORBIT. This means the joystick position is permanently glued off-center, leading to continuous spinning, unlike NEON ORBIT, which self-centers smoothly. This may be the reason STARFALL feels awkward. I\'m noting that STARFALL\'s stick sensitivity is closely linked to viewport pixels. I\'m focusing on the effect on user experience.\n\n\n**Calculating Mouse Sensitivity**\n\nI\'m now carefully calculating NEON ORBIT\'s mouse-to-stick sensitivity compared to STARFALL, particularly focusing on how aspect ratio affects the user experience. The analysis reveals STARFALL\'s sensitivity is far higher than NEON ORBIT\'s: 1.56x twitchier horizontally and 2.38x twitchier vertically. Even worse, the horizontal sensitivity changes with the aspect ratio, which is awful. This may explain why STARFALL feels so awkward to control.\n\n\n**Analyzing Sensitivity Discrepancies**\n\nI\'m now investigating the nonlinear response curve and sensitivity differences between NEON ORBIT and STARFALL, particularly how warped vertical pitch felt compared to yaw. In NEON ORBIT, the mouse sensitivity is consistent, ensuring identical pixels-per-deflection regardless of aspect ratio or screen dimensions. However, STARFALL\'s response curve introduces a deadzone, followed by a stacked nonlinear response, making it highly unpredictable, which could explain the awkward feel.\n\n\n**Examining Sensitivity Scaling**\n\nI\'m now comparing the mouse sensitivity scaling between the two games. I can see that in STARFALL, the pitch and yaw sensitivities are multiplied by `profile.yawSpeed` and `profile.pitchSpeed`, adding another layer of sensitivity on top of the already non-linear response. In NEON ORBIT, the mapping from mouse input to control is clean, linear, and 1:1, so it provides proportional micro-steering. I am making an assessment of how the multiple scaling factors impact control.\n\n\n\n**Evaluating Aim Consistency**\n\nI\'m now carefully examining the NEON ORBIT aiming system. The core concept is straightforward: a self-centering virtual stick ensures the ship\'s nose aligns with the firing line when the stick returns to center. I need to verify that bolts fire directly from the muzzles along the ship\'s forward axis, with the reticle precisely at the projected boresight, which requires testing. I also want to compare with a subtle gimbal or pure boresight for the best combat feel.\n\n\n**Verifying Aiming Systems**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the aiming system, specifically the integration of the self-centering virtual stick with the ship\'s firing line. I\'m verifying that the bolts fire along the ship\'s true forward vector, and that the reticle tracks where the nose is actually pointing. I\'m confirming that the boresight aligns perfectly, ensuring the combat feel is crisp, as expected. I\'m also examining the code for flight and camera controls, to find inconsistencies and areas for improvement.\n\n\n**Calculating Self-Center Dynamics**\n\nI\'m now calculating the impact of variable frame-rate and exponential decay on the stick\'s self-centering behavior. I\'ve found discrepancies between the NEON ORBIT and STARFALL control systems. The analysis revealed that STARFALL\'s application of `dt` in `InputManager` coupled with `FlightController`\'s damping could introduce instability under variable frame rates. I am now formulating a stable model.\n\n\n**Modeling Stick Centering**\n\nI\'m now formulating a model for the stick\'s self-centering behavior, accounting for variable frame-rates. My calculations show the steady-state error is within ~1% across a range of 30\342\200\223144 FPS. I can further improve the stability with half-step decay or fixed-timestep sub-stepping methods. Using the midpoint splitting method, the steady state error at 30 FPS is $O((\\lambda \\Delta t)^2)$.\n\n\n**Refining Self-Centering Model**\n\nI\'m now refining the self-centering model, focusing on performance at lower frame rates. The Strang splitting method provides outstanding performance and accuracy. My calculations reveal the total integrated turn angle is virtually identical across 30, 60, and 144 FPS. I also observed the benefit of using exact exponential integration of the virtual stick to keep the physics completely frame-rate-independent.\n\n\n**Validating Exponential Stability**\n\nI\'m now deeply immersed in validating the exponential stability of STARFALL\'s control system, and I\'m very excited by my findings. I\'ve found an incredibly precise and stable self-centering model by using the Strang splitting method. My analysis reveals a minimal steady-state error even at variable frame rates. This rigorous analysis has revealed how `dt` values and clamping affect stability. I\'m now testing the model.\n\n\n**Testing Frame-Rate Handling**\n\nI\'m now investigating how NEON ORBIT\'s input system handles variable frame rates. The inspection confirmed a crucial detail: the asynchronous `mousemove` event updates the virtual stick, and then a 60 Hz tick decays that value. The combination of `Math.exp(-STICK_RECENTRE * dt)` in the flight controller and `useFrame` works effectively, even with variable `dt`, leading to an incredibly stable control experience. I\'m taking notes on how this system adapts to different frame rates.\n\n\n**Refining Input System**\n\nI\'m now refining STARFALL\'s input system, focusing on stable self-centering at variable frame rates. Calculations confirm that symmetric half-step decay, as used in NEON ORBIT, provides stability at different frame rates. The total decay remains constant over time. The clamp and sensitivity calculations have been verified. I\'m noting the difference between 30 and 144 FPS.\n\n\n**Examining Frame-Rate Effects**\n\nI\'m now carefully examining the effects of variable frame rates and ship rotation integration within NEON ORBIT. My investigation confirmed that angular velocity tracks the target angular velocity with exponential damping, including angular acceleration ceiling. The analysis validates NEON ORBIT\'s approach: direct rotation and controlled angular acceleration/damping. I\'m taking notes on how this translates to STARFALL.\n\n\n**Preserving Design Choices**\n\nI\'m now carefully examining the NEON ORBIT codebase, and I have validated the intended control behaviors, especially the virtual stick and smooth self-centering. I am also making sure to adhere to the STARFALL requirements. The goal is now to incorporate the NEON ORBIT architecture, while preserving the key design choices. My top priorities are to keep the Pointer Lock implementation, the 6-DoF arcade gameplay, and the third-person chase camera.\n\n\n**Verifying Virtual Stick Deflection**\n\nI\'m verifying that the virtual stick\'s deflection limit in NEON ORBIT is independent of viewport pixels and any visual HUD display. The analysis confirms a normalized stick space of `[-maxDeflection, +maxDeflection]` per axis. I am now noting that it\'s driven by `movementX * mouseSensitivity`, where `mouseSensitivity = 1 / 300`, and that\'s an isotropic 300 px for full deflection. I\'m focusing on separating the logical stick from any visual HUD.\n\n\n**Refining Virtual Stick Display**\n\nI\'m now integrating an enhanced HUD, decoupling the logical stick from any visual representation. The current plan includes a crisp cannon boresight reticle and a subtle circular stick-deflection indicator, whose radius will map to the internal stick values. I\'ve decided to replace the rectangular indicator with a cleaner circular design for a more professional and physics-accurate feel.\n\n\n**Calibrating Control Parameters**\n\nI\'m now extracting and calibrating sensitivity values from NEON ORBIT. My goal is to ensure STARFALL\'s parameters match the reference. Specifically, I\'ve confirmed `MOUSE_SENSITIVITY = 1/300`, `STICK_RECENTRE = 1.6`, maximum stick deflection `1.0`, and a zero deadzone. I\'m taking notes on how these parameters translate into the gameplay experience.\n\n\n**Validating Parameter Matching**\n\nI\'m now confirming the 0.0 deadzone setting, as it perfectly matches NEON ORBIT. My investigation into ship turn rates reveals that the Interceptor is a direct match, with the same values. After some tweaking, I\'m now matching the hornet and drone as well! These new default values offer identical control feel, and I\'m ensuring all values are now configurable.\n\n\n**Examining Sensitivity Settings**\n\nI\'m now investigating how adjusting STARFALL\'s sensitivity settings affects the core gameplay. I\'m focusing on the impact of `yawSpeed` and `pitchSpeed` in the context of the enemy AI and missions. I need to ensure that modifications to these values don\'t break existing game mechanics. It seems I should keep NEON ORBIT\'s default values for balance, so my next step is to examine the impact of angular acceleration and damping.\n\n\n\n**Refining Angular Velocity**\n\nI\'m now carefully optimizing angular velocity, focusing on the sensitivity discrepancies and smoothing. My current work examines the impact of `angularDamping` and `angularAcceleration` on tracking and responsiveness. I\'m aiming for high `angularDamping` to prevent mushiness, while balancing `angularAcceleration` to avoid clipping. The goal is to provide a 1:1, responsive feel, but with clean micro-smoothing.\n\n\n**Comparing Camera Lag**\n\nI\'m now investigating camera control in STARFALL versus NEON ORBIT, particularly the effects of lag and follow settings. I\'ve noted the artificial lag offsets introduced in STARFALL\'s `CameraController` compared to NEON ORBIT, which leverages natural position and rotation parameters for a cleaner feel. The NEON ORBIT approach seems more elegant, and the next step is to examine how these parameters work.\n\n\n**Refining Camera Lag**\n\nI\'m now diving into the details of camera lag in NEON ORBIT. The current work analyzes how `lagX = -flight.yawVelocity * 0.5` affects the natural turn lag! The line of code is fascinating. I\'m noting the camera is shifted `+X` for a right turn and `-X` for a left turn, which moves the camera INSIDE the turn. I\'m testing the result now!\n\n\n**Grasping Camera Control**\n\nI\'m now deep in the camera controls, comparing lag in STARFALL versus NEON ORBIT. The NEON ORBIT code reveals an elegant approach: a purely behind-the-ship camera offset combined with natural lag, without fighting the ship\'s turns. My investigations uncovered a subtle camera bug: STARFALL\'s camera shifts the view to the outside of the turn, instead of inside. I\'m now grepping the codebase.\n\n\n"
