  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Code Structure**\n\nI\'m impressed by the codebase\'s cleanliness and modularity. I\'ve noted how steering logic is neatly isolated in `src/config/steering.ts` (for config) and `src/config/ships.ts` (for ship profiles and persistence), which are great. This separation is key to maintainability. The file name conventions also adhere to a consistent structure.\n\n\n**Refining Settings Migration**\n\nI\'m now focused on the pre-v7 settings migration. I\'m ensuring a clean transition to the NEON ORBIT-calibrated defaults while preserving critical settings like audio, graphics, camera, and invertY. I\'ve been looking at the structure of `src/types/game.ts`, particularly `GameSettings` and `FlightTelemetry`. The `InputManager.ts` implementation is really well-done, with its pointer lock, relative movement, and independent virtual stick state. Also, the FlightController.ts is great!\n\n\n**Designing Architecture Rigorously**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into architecture. I\'m focusing on the strict separation between `InputManager` and `FlightController`, as specified. The FlightHUD and SettingsPanel also look fantastic. I am excited to look at the crisp 3D-projected cannon boresight reticle and the clean decoupled virtual-stick controls. The clear separation of concerns in input handling, flight physics, and user interface elements is promising. I am making sure to think through every edge case.\n\n\n**Defining Input Boundaries**\n\nI am clarifying the design constraints for the `InputManager`. I will ensure the stick smoothly returns to center when the mouse stops, with the ship\'s turn rate dictating actual rotation. Maximum deflection is bounded, and the ship will continue turning as long as the stick is deflected. Returning the stick towards center must smoothly reduce turning. The mouse input and flight physics will be kept separate. Also, I will not stack additional sensitivity curves without a demonstrated reason. I will also preserve the fixed Pointer Lock implementation from STARFALL, including the ownership checks. Continuing to use `movementX` and `movementY`, no cursor recentering. Physical screen edges must never interrupt input.\n\n\n**Implementing Input Logic**\n\nI\'ve been working on the implementation of synchronous `requestPointerLock()` within a click gesture. I\'m focusing on handling `event.movementX` and `event.movementY` exclusively, and maintaining the warp spike guard. I\'m ensuring proper virtual stick resets on lock/unlock, blur, deactivation, and reset events. I\'m separating the virtual stick state from flight physics, using normalized deflection units, and accumulating raw deltas for application in `consumeSnapshot`. This approach seems superior for its decoupling.\n\n\n**Verifying Implementation Rigor**\n\nI\'m verifying the input logic, especially concerning the separation of concerns. I\'ve realized that the current approach guarantees the `InputManager` doesn\'t need stale copies of settings, as the live settings passed in each tick are immediately effective. This is elegant. Now I\'m comparing Strang splitting decay to exact NEON ORBIT decay, and how the sensitivity and deflection settings interact. I am making sure that frame rates affect the behavior as little as possible. The approach also preserves `mouseDeltaX` and `mouseDeltaY` for diagnostics.\n\n\n**Testing Input Calculations**\n\nI\'m now deep-diving into the input calculations, focusing specifically on the half-step decay and mouse movement application. I need to comprehensively test the formula with various input scenarios, like small and large mouse movements, to ensure accurate stick behavior and smooth decay. This involves checking if the stick returns to zero precisely, after input, and analyzing extreme cases.\n\n\n**Validating Input Formulas**\n\nI am now thoroughly validating the input formulas, particularly the decay and mouse movement integration. I\'m excited by the frame-rate independence; the half-decay and exponential decay is exactly what\'s needed. I\'m testing various movement scenarios and decay rates, analyzing the resulting ship rotation, confirming accurate stick behavior, and smooth decay and I\'m ensuring there are no numerical artifacts. I\'m also now analyzing a fast sweep with 600 px/s, just to make sure I\'m really hitting all the edge cases.\n\n\n**Verifying Stick Behavior**\n\nI\'m now focusing on verifying the virtual stick\'s behavior, especially the interaction between deflection and self-centering. I\'m confirming that reaching maximum deflection is clean and the maximum turn rate is held. I\'m verifying that the return-to-center functionality smoothly reduces turning. I\'m also ensuring that the system is properly decoupled, so that holding the stick deflected (without additional mouse movement) produces continuous turning.\n\n\n**Confirming Ship Turning**\n\nI\'m now confirming the ship\'s continuous turning behavior. I can see that the ship will continuously turn at a rate dictated by `deflection * turnRate` if the stick is deflected, whether from a fast or slow mouse sweep, while decaying back, or by arrow key input. Upon mouse stop, the stick smoothly centers and the turning smoothly tapers. I\'m ensuring no physical screen edges interrupt this. I am validating that deadzones are handled via clean linear rescaling, without any stacked curves, especially for radial zones.\n\n\n\n**Refining Deadzone Logic**\n\nI am now refining the deadzone logic, and ensuring proper 1:1 mapping when the deadzone is zero. My formulas are clean, which is exactly how NEON ORBIT\'s input handled it. I am also checking the combination of the mouse virtual stick and keyboard arrow keys for both yaw and pitch, ensuring these handle correctly using the same strategy as in NEON ORBIT. I am ensuring that the `keyYaw` and `keyPitch` variables correctly map to nose right, nose left, nose up and nose down, as specified in `src/core/input.ts`.\n\n\n**Defining Steering Calculations**\n\nI\'m now carefully defining the steering calculations. I\'m focusing on aligning the key bindings, the mouse pitch sign, and how I\'ll combine the inputs. I am checking the STARFALL key bindings carefully and ensuring consistency between `keyPitchUp`, `keyPitchDown`, `keyYawLeft`, and `keyYawRight` with the rest of the controls. I will ensure a smooth transformation to steering values, taking into account `invertY`, `stickOutputX` and `stickOutputY`, `keyYaw` and `keyPitch`, and `maxDeflection`.\n\n\n\n**Tracing Flight Physics**\n\nI\'m now tracing the flight physics in `FlightController.update()`. I\'m verifying the input-to-velocity calculation, specifically how `steerYaw` and `steerPitch` are used to compute `targetYawVel` and `targetPitchVel`. I\'m confirming that the sign conventions for ship rotation align correctly with the expected behavior, like a right mouse movement leading to a left yaw. I\'m also now verifying the use of `bound` helper.\n\n\n\n**Validating Sign Conventions**\n\nI\'m now thoroughly validating sign conventions within the flight physics, ensuring everything aligns perfectly. I\'ve confirmed that the mouse movements, combined with `invertY`, the stick output, steer values, and target velocities all produce the correct ship rotations. I am also validating the sign for roll directions, verifying the correct direction is used, based on Three.js\'s rotation and roll keybindings. I am checking the exact roll of the ship.\n\n\n\n**Verifying Config Consistency**\n\nI\'m now carefully verifying the consistency between NEON ORBIT\'s parameters and STARFALL\'s in `STEERING_CONFIG`. I\'m confirming that `baseMouseSensitivity`, `stickSelfCenterRate`, `deadzone`, `maxStickDeflection`, `yawTurnRate`, and `pitchTurnRate` are correctly aligned. I\'m focusing on the `angularAcceleration` and `angularDamping` and their roles in creating smooth, responsive controls. I\'m making sure these controls feel great, by trying to feel this by comparing to NEON ORBIT.\n\n\n\n**Calculating Steering Physics**\n\nI\'m now carefully calculating the steering physics. I\'ve focused on confirming the ship physics parameters. Using `angularDamping = 26.0` and `angularAcceleration = 36.0`, I get the expected decay and settling behavior. I calculated the step responses and am now considering the agility parameters, checking the Interceptor values. I\'m verifying the 95% target turn rate in approximately 0.11 seconds and the clean settling without overshoot. I\'m now testing angular decay with the decay rate parameters.\n\n\n**Refining Ship Parameters**\n\nI\'m now refining ship parameters to match NEON ORBIT. The Wasp, Hornet, and Drone classes align perfectly. Local-space quaternion rotation is confirmed. Right-multiplying provides pure 6-DoF flight with no gimbal lock. Weapon aiming now fires directly along the ship\'s boresight, eliminating jarring gimbal issues and restoring predictable target leading. This should significantly improve the gameplay feel.\n\n\n**Optimizing Camera Dynamics**\n\nI\'m now diving into the `CameraController.ts`, carefully removing artificial lag and tuning the follow rates to match NEON ORBIT\'s balance. I am ensuring that the position lags smoothly while rotation stays crisply ahead, providing authentic feel. I am checking `posLambda` and `rotLambda`, to confirm that the numbers here are correct.\n\n\n\n**Analyzing HUD Indicators**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the flight HUD visuals. I am integrating STARFALL\'s smooth `currentUp` tracking, along with max allowed distance, boost pull-back and boost FOV. I am also planning to implement combat impact tremor. For the boresight, I will ensure it includes hit confirmation X, kill confirmation burst, and boost/firing ring feedback, and all are accurately positioned, and that the decoupled virtual stick indicators use a centered circular ring with tick marks and a proportionally moving stick pip.\n\n\n\n**Implementing Virtual Stick**\n\nI\'m now implementing the virtual stick controls with visual feedback. The stick pip now smoothly glides back to center when the mouse stops, visually confirming self-centering. I am using `data-*` attributes for diagnostics and Playwright testing. I\'m focusing on radial clamping and internal axis bounding.\n\n\n**Validating Stick Feedback**\n\nI\'m now carefully validating the virtual stick behavior with visual feedback. The stick pip is correctly returning to center when the mouse is released. I\'m focusing on ensuring smooth visual transitions. Radial clamping is solid, with internal axis bounding working perfectly. The `data-*` attributes facilitate easy diagnostics and thorough Playwright testing.\n\n\n\n**Evaluating Code Removal**\n\nI am now assessing the necessity of `AimBoundary.ts`. I\'ve confirmed, through `grep`, that only `FlightController`, `FlightHUD`, and `SettingsPanel` import it. This suggests a potential for removal. I am ensuring that no other files, including tests and scripts, have dependencies on this file. I am verifying the proper key and calibrated defaults for virtual-stick. This will include the removal of legacy widgets, such as the obsolete joystick widgets.\n\n\n\n"
