  user_input {
    user_response: "# STARFALL EMERGENCY FLIGHT FEEL RECOVERY\n## Arcade Space Combat Control \342\200\224 Stabilization Pass\n\nYou have modified STARFALL\'s flight and mouse control systems through multiple iterations.\n\nThe current flight feel is still unsatisfactory.\n\nThis is a stabilization and recovery task.\n\nDo not redesign the game.\nDo not introduce another experimental control architecture.\nDo not rewrite the entire flight controller.\n\nThe target is a responsive, predictable arcade space combat flight model.\n\n## 1. Target Control Feel\n\nI explicitly choose:\n\n**B \342\200\224 Traditional Arcade Space Combat**\n\nThe desired experience:\n\n- Direct and responsive mouse steering.\n- Low input latency.\n- Predictable yaw and pitch.\n- Smooth continuous turning.\n- Easy target tracking.\n- Precise small corrections.\n- Fast large directional changes.\n- No sluggish virtual joystick behavior.\n- No excessive inertia.\n- No excessive smoothing.\n- No unexpected acceleration or sensitivity spikes.\n\nThe ship should feel connected to the mouse.\n\nSmall movements should produce small, controlled turns.\n\nLarger movements should produce stronger turns.\n\nThe player must be able to track moving targets naturally.\n\n## 2. Freeze the Existing Architecture First\n\nBefore changing code:\n\n- Inspect the current mouse input implementation.\n- Inspect the current flight controller.\n- Inspect the current camera controller.\n- Inspect all sensitivity and smoothing configuration.\n- Review the recent changes that affected flight feel.\n\nIdentify which systems are currently responsible for:\n\n1. Mouse input collection.\n2. Input normalization.\n3. Sensitivity scaling.\n4. Ship yaw and pitch.\n5. Angular velocity.\n6. Angular damping.\n7. Camera follow.\n\nDo not replace working systems simply because another architecture seems cleaner.\n\nPreserve the existing architecture wherever possible.\n\n## 3. Remove Unwanted Control Behavior\n\nInvestigate and correct:\n\n- Excessive virtual joystick self-centering.\n- Excessive deadzone.\n- Excessive input smoothing.\n- Duplicated sensitivity multipliers.\n- Conflicting input accumulation.\n- Excessive angular inertia.\n- Delayed camera response.\n- Unexpected sensitivity acceleration.\n- Input being processed more than once.\n\nDo not add more layers of control processing.\n\nUse the simplest reliable input-to-flight mapping that achieves the target feel.\n\n## 4. Restore Direct Arcade Mouse Steering\n\nImplement or restore this behavior:\n\n- Relative mouse movement directly controls yaw and pitch steering intent.\n- Use `movementX` and `movementY`.\n- Apply one centralized sensitivity multiplier.\n- Use a controlled maximum angular velocity.\n- Apply only enough smoothing to prevent abrupt or unstable motion.\n- Keep horizontal and vertical sensitivity independently adjustable.\n- Ensure the response remains consistent at different frame rates.\n\nAvoid requiring large mouse movements to initiate turning.\n\nAvoid requiring the player to fight virtual joystick recentering.\n\nDo not make the ship continue rotating uncontrollably after the player stops providing steering input.\n\n## 5. Aim Boundary\n\nKeep the current aim boundary configurable.\n\nUse a conservative initial region:\n\n- Width: 30% of viewport width.\n- Height: 35% of viewport height.\n- Shape: Rounded rectangle.\n- Position: Centered.\n\nDo not let boundary size create excessive sensitivity.\n\nThe boundary should define the aiming region, not introduce another hidden sensitivity multiplier.\n\nIf the existing boundary implementation conflicts with direct arcade steering, simplify its interaction with the flight controller without breaking continuous mouse input.\n\n## 6. Preserve the Working Pointer Lock Fix\n\nThis is critical.\n\nThe physical mouse edge rollback issue was already fixed.\n\nPreserve that successful fix.\n\n- Keep Pointer Lock.\n- Keep relative mouse input.\n- Never introduce cursor recentering.\n- Never depend on absolute cursor coordinates.\n- Physical display edges must not interrupt steering.\n- Do not process the same input twice.\n\nDo not rewrite this system unless a specific technical defect is demonstrated.\n\n## 7. Camera Behavior\n\nThe third-person chase camera must:\n\n- Follow the ship smoothly.\n- Respond quickly to directional changes.\n- Avoid excessive trailing.\n- Avoid competing with ship rotation.\n- Preserve the existing cinematic presentation.\n\nCamera smoothing must not make flight controls feel delayed.\n\n## 8. Recovery Procedure\n\nMake this a controlled recovery pass.\n\n1. Inspect the current implementation and recent modifications.\n2. Identify the main causes of poor handling.\n3. Restore or correct the relevant parameters.\n4. Make the smallest set of necessary changes.\n5. Test the actual browser game.\n6. Compare the result with the previous Phase 1 flight prototype if available.\n\nDo not make unrelated feature changes.\n\nDo not change combat, weapons, bots, missions, progression, or visual design.\n\n## 9. Acceptance Tests\n\nThe task is complete only when the actual browser game passes these tests:\n\n- Small mouse movements produce precise turns.\n- Larger movements produce stronger turns.\n- The ship responds immediately.\n- No excessive input lag.\n- No unwanted drifting after input stops.\n- No uncontrollable rotation.\n- Smooth and predictable target tracking.\n- Stable camera follow.\n- Consistent behavior at different frame rates.\n- Pointer Lock continues working at physical screen edges.\n- Boost remains functional.\n\n## 10. Final Report\n\nReport:\n\n1. Root causes of the poor flight feel.\n2. Files changed.\n3. Which old behaviors were removed or corrected.\n4. Final sensitivity and angular velocity settings.\n5. How input latency was controlled.\n6. Actual browser test results.\n7. Remaining problems.\n\nDo not claim that the handling is fixed unless you have tested it in the browser.\n\n**Priority: Restore a responsive, precise, enjoyable arcade space combat flight experience with the smallest possible changes.**"
    items {
      text: "# STARFALL EMERGENCY FLIGHT FEEL RECOVERY\n## Arcade Space Combat Control \342\200\224 Stabilization Pass\n\nYou have modified STARFALL\'s flight and mouse control systems through multiple iterations.\n\nThe current flight feel is still unsatisfactory.\n\nThis is a stabilization and recovery task.\n\nDo not redesign the game.\nDo not introduce another experimental control architecture.\nDo not rewrite the entire flight controller.\n\nThe target is a responsive, predictable arcade space combat flight model.\n\n## 1. Target Control Feel\n\nI explicitly choose:\n\n**B \342\200\224 Traditional Arcade Space Combat**\n\nThe desired experience:\n\n- Direct and responsive mouse steering.\n- Low input latency.\n- Predictable yaw and pitch.\n- Smooth continuous turning.\n- Easy target tracking.\n- Precise small corrections.\n- Fast large directional changes.\n- No sluggish virtual joystick behavior.\n- No excessive inertia.\n- No excessive smoothing.\n- No unexpected acceleration or sensitivity spikes.\n\nThe ship should feel connected to the mouse.\n\nSmall movements should produce small, controlled turns.\n\nLarger movements should produce stronger turns.\n\nThe player must be able to track moving targets naturally.\n\n## 2. Freeze the Existing Architecture First\n\nBefore changing code:\n\n- Inspect the current mouse input implementation.\n- Inspect the current flight controller.\n- Inspect the current camera controller.\n- Inspect all sensitivity and smoothing configuration.\n- Review the recent changes that affected flight feel.\n\nIdentify which systems are currently responsible for:\n\n1. Mouse input collection.\n2. Input normalization.\n3. Sensitivity scaling.\n4. Ship yaw and pitch.\n5. Angular velocity.\n6. Angular damping.\n7. Camera follow.\n\nDo not replace working systems simply because another architecture seems cleaner.\n\nPreserve the existing architecture wherever possible.\n\n## 3. Remove Unwanted Control Behavior\n\nInvestigate and correct:\n\n- Excessive virtual joystick self-centering.\n- Excessive deadzone.\n- Excessive input smoothing.\n- Duplicated sensitivity multipliers.\n- Conflicting input accumulation.\n- Excessive angular inertia.\n- Delayed camera response.\n- Unexpected sensitivity acceleration.\n- Input being processed more than once.\n\nDo not add more layers of control processing.\n\nUse the simplest reliable input-to-flight mapping that achieves the target feel.\n\n## 4. Restore Direct Arcade Mouse Steering\n\nImplement or restore this behavior:\n\n- Relative mouse movement directly controls yaw and pitch steering intent.\n- Use `movementX` and `movementY`.\n- Apply one centralized sensitivity multiplier.\n- Use a controlled maximum angular velocity.\n- Apply only enough smoothing to prevent abrupt or unstable motion.\n- Keep horizontal and vertical sensitivity independently adjustable.\n- Ensure the response remains consistent at different frame rates.\n\nAvoid requiring large mouse movements to initiate turning.\n\nAvoid requiring the player to fight virtual joystick recentering.\n\nDo not make the ship continue rotating uncontrollably after the player stops providing steering input.\n\n## 5. Aim Boundary\n\nKeep the current aim boundary configurable.\n\nUse a conservative initial region:\n\n- Width: 30% of viewport width.\n- Height: 35% of viewport height.\n- Shape: Rounded rectangle.\n- Position: Centered.\n\nDo not let boundary size create excessive sensitivity.\n\nThe boundary should define the aiming region, not introduce another hidden sensitivity multiplier.\n\nIf the existing boundary implementation conflicts with direct arcade steering, simplify its interaction with the flight controller without breaking continuous mouse input.\n\n## 6. Preserve the Working Pointer Lock Fix\n\nThis is critical.\n\nThe physical mouse edge rollback issue was already fixed.\n\nPreserve that successful fix.\n\n- Keep Pointer Lock.\n- Keep relative mouse input.\n- Never introduce cursor recentering.\n- Never depend on absolute cursor coordinates.\n- Physical display edges must not interrupt steering.\n- Do not process the same input twice.\n\nDo not rewrite this system unless a specific technical defect is demonstrated.\n\n## 7. Camera Behavior\n\nThe third-person chase camera must:\n\n- Follow the ship smoothly.\n- Respond quickly to directional changes.\n- Avoid excessive trailing.\n- Avoid competing with ship rotation.\n- Preserve the existing cinematic presentation.\n\nCamera smoothing must not make flight controls feel delayed.\n\n## 8. Recovery Procedure\n\nMake this a controlled recovery pass.\n\n1. Inspect the current implementation and recent modifications.\n2. Identify the main causes of poor handling.\n3. Restore or correct the relevant parameters.\n4. Make the smallest set of necessary changes.\n5. Test the actual browser game.\n6. Compare the result with the previous Phase 1 flight prototype if available.\n\nDo not make unrelated feature changes.\n\nDo not change combat, weapons, bots, missions, progression, or visual design.\n\n## 9. Acceptance Tests\n\nThe task is complete only when the actual browser game passes these tests:\n\n- Small mouse movements produce precise turns.\n- Larger movements produce stronger turns.\n- The ship responds immediately.\n- No excessive input lag.\n- No unwanted drifting after input stops.\n- No uncontrollable rotation.\n- Smooth and predictable target tracking.\n- Stable camera follow.\n- Consistent behavior at different frame rates.\n- Pointer Lock continues working at physical screen edges.\n- Boost remains functional.\n\n## 10. Final Report\n\nReport:\n\n1. Root causes of the poor flight feel.\n2. Files changed.\n3. Which old behaviors were removed or corrected.\n4. Final sensitivity and angular velocity settings.\n5. How input latency was controlled.\n6. Actual browser test results.\n7. Remaining problems.\n\nDo not claim that the handling is fixed unless you have tested it in the browser.\n\n**Priority: Restore a responsive, precise, enjoyable arcade space combat flight experience with the smallest possible changes.**"
