    thinking: "**Clarifying Flight Recovery**\n\nNow it is all crystal clear! I see how cleanly everything fits together and how surgical the stabilization and recovery pass can be. I\'ll check `package.json` and `/ms-playwright` to understand our test tooling before changing any code. I\'m moving toward designing and implementing the exact flight recovery now.\n\n\n**Designing Mouse Steering Math**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the precise math for direct mouse steering in the arcade combat model. I want to make certain that direct mouse movements translate precisely into yaw and pitch. The requirements are extremely specific, ensuring no drift, no deadzones, and linear responsiveness. I\'m focusing on the direct mapping of mouse delta to steering intent, eliminating any virtual joystick accumulation.\n\n\n**Refining Sensitivity Parameters**\n\nI\'m now concentrating on tuning the sensitivity parameters for precise mouse steering. Specifically, I\'m proposing a single centralized physical scale within `STEERING_CONFIG`. This scale, `baseRadiansPerPixel = 0.0028` rad/px, allows for fine-grained adjustments, like a 1.6-degree micro-adjustment for a 10-pixel nudge. I\'ve been comparing the proposed value with successful implementations in similar games like Everspace 2 and Chorus to find a comfortable balance between precision and responsiveness.\n\n\n**Calculating Mouse-Turn Speed**\n\nI am now doing the math on various mouse movements, from small corrections to rapid flicks, to establish precise mappings between mouse input and ship rotation. I\'ve determined that a baseline of `0.0032` rad/px gives a responsive feel. I\'m also now analyzing the resulting angular velocities to ensure these match the Interceptor\'s maximum yaw speed, which I will test at different settings. I think the flick speed will need to be clamped.\n\n\n**Analyzing Frame-Rate Sensitivity**\n\nI\'m now carefully analyzing the impact of different frame rates (30, 60, 120, 144 FPS) on the conversion of mouse delta into angular velocity. My focus is on calculating `safeDt` for each frame rate, so I can accurately express `mouseDeltaX` at each `safeDt`. I am confident that by clamping the requested angular velocity to the controlled ship ceiling, I can create consistent and stable rotation at all frame rates. I am now deep in the critical math!\n\n\n**Investigating Impulse Response**\n\nI\'m now investigating the integral of `yawVelocity * dt` after a single mouse impulse, examining the effects of light exponential smoothing across different frame rates. The math is complex, but I\'m looking at how a single `mouseDeltaX` on frame zero propagates. Specifically, I\'m analyzing the values at 30, 60, and 144 FPS to confirm frame-rate independence. My calculations are focusing on a single impulse and subsequent zero input to understand the smoothing\'s effect.\n\n\n**Verifying Impulse Math**\n\nThe math has all fallen into place now! I\'ve confirmed that with an impulse, there is *exact* frame-rate independence! No matter the frame rate, the total angle rotated from a single mouse impulse is precisely `-D * k`. I\'m going to double-check my algebra one more time, but I am close to complete!\n\n\n**Eliminating FPS Artifacts**\n\nI\'ve just made a critical discovery! I now see how frame rate and mouse polling interact in a way that would make high FPS feel slower, due to clipping. I\'m focused on precisely calculating how to create a mathematically smooth, FPS-independent mapping. I\'m evaluating unclamped velocity, applying smoothing, and then capping the output. Now I need to design this in detail.\n\n\n**Calculating Smoothing Behavior**\n\nI\'m now calculating the precise behavior of the exponential smoothing, focusing on how different frame rates affect the responsiveness of the ship\'s rotation. I\'m verifying that the smoothing parameters provide a natural feel at different frame rates. The math is complex, but I\'m getting close to a solid understanding of how it will behave. I need to make sure the smoothing parameter, tau, is well tuned.\n\n\n**Confirming Velocity Clamping**\n\nI\'ve just confirmed the velocity clamping across different frame rates, specifically 60 and 144 FPS, calculating that a small movement of 16 pixels results in an identical rotation of 2.93 degrees in both scenarios. For a fast flick, the clamped angular velocity is consistent and bounded, ensuring the ship never spins out of control. The decay rate of `yawVelocity` after the mouse stops is also as expected. I\'m now working to compare even faster stopping versus silky smoothness.\n\n\n**Verifying Precision Math**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on confirming the precision of a single clean `lambda` at 28.0/s. I\'ve found that it gives exact mathematical frame-rate invariance, so the `\316\243 v_n dt = -D * k` is accurate to floating-point precision at multiple frame rates. Using just a single clean `lambda` will give ultra-low latency, and silky smoothing.\n\n\n**Analyzing Key Blending**\n\nI\'m now integrating key input for clean blending. I see that arrow keys can blend smoothly with mouse control using `keyYaw` and `keyPitch` rates. I\'m focusing on incorporating these rates with existing mouse-derived velocities, clamping the final angular velocities to ensure precise control. This approach should prevent uncontrollable spins while providing blended control from both inputs.\n\n\n**Evaluating Ship Rotation Rates**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on ship rotation rates, specifically, the Interceptor, Striker, and Destroyer. I\'ve calculated maximum yaw and pitch rates for each ship based on provided parameters, which are Interceptor: 162\302\260/s yaw and 151\302\260/s pitch, Striker: 2.43 rad/s yaw and 2.30 rad/s pitch, and Destroyer: 1.89 rad/s yaw and 1.78 rad/s pitch. I will evaluate the feel with the current settings. I\'m also considering whether to scale the `dampingLambda` by ship agility or keep it crisp.\n\n\n**Calibrating Sensitivity Fine-Tuning**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on calibrating the sensitivity parameters. I\'m focusing on the `baseRadiansPerPixel` value and its implications. I\'ve calculated that with 0.0032 rad/px and the provided mouse movement examples, even small adjustments will give appropriate turning. I will soon be evaluating the feel with the current settings. I\'ll test the slider range to determine what feels most natural.\n\n\n\n**Designing Aim Boundary Constraints**\n\nI\'m now carefully designing the aim boundary, ensuring it only defines an aiming region without impacting sensitivity. I\'m focusing on its configurable properties (width, height, corner radius) and their interaction with the flight controller, specifically ensuring it doesn\'t introduce another sensitivity multiplier. My goal is to keep the mouse input continuous and simplify interactions. I\'m taking the recovery brief Section 5 into account.\n\n\n**Refining Aim Boundary Logic**\n\nI am now focused on integrating a pure geometric aim boundary with direct arcade steering, ensuring a clean separation of concerns. I\'m starting by recreating a minimal `/src/engine/AimBoundary.ts` file that manages just the rounded rectangle geometry, no input curves or virtual joystick math. Then, I\'ll update the `FlightController` so that steering is purely driven by `mouseDelta` scaled by a base sensitivity, while the `aimBoundaryScale` parameter will only control the visual aiming region\'s size, having no effect on the turning rate or sensitivity. My aim is to create visual HUD feedback.\n\n\n**Visualizing Clean Aim Geometry**\n\nI\'m now visualizing how smoothly my design integrates the aiming reticle\'s rounded rectangle with the flight controller\'s direct steering. I\'m focusing on ensuring seamless behavior during straight flight, smooth turns within the boundary, continuous mouse rotation, and accurate weapon firing along the ship\'s boresight. I can see the aim reticle perfectly aligned with the ship when the mouse is still, leading during turns, and the weapon firing exactly forward, uninterrupted, always.\n\n\n**Verifying Laser Alignment**\n\nI\'m now confirming the exact forward boresight for all laser shots, ensuring perfect alignment with the ship\'s nose using `_FORWARD_LOCAL` and `this.quaternion`. This eliminates any discrepancy between the ship\'s pointing direction and laser trajectory. The HUD reticle will align perfectly on the ship\'s aim convergence. I\'m focusing on the implementation to guarantee perfect weapon tracking.\n\n\n\n**Adjusting Camera Follow**\n\nNow I\'m focusing on the camera\'s behavior, specifically the chase responsiveness and subtle lateral lag. I\'ve adjusted `posLambda`, `upAlpha`, and `rotLambda` for a more responsive follow, and reduced the camera\'s guardrail to `maxAllowedDist`. I\'m also clamping `lagX` and `lagY` to create a natural, outside-arc feel.\n\n\n**Simplifying Game Configuration**\n\nI am now focusing on cleaning up the configuration files, particularly `src/config/ships.ts`, `src/types/game.ts`, `src/components/SettingsPanel.tsx`, and `src/components/FlightHUD.tsx`. I am removing the virtual joystick artifacts and introducing a set of clean, direct arcade steering parameters: a single constant, a few sensitivities, a smoothing factor, and a clamping parameter. I\'m taking care to keep the camera settings untouched. The goal is a simpler, less confusing configuration.\n\n\n**Refining Boundary Integration**\n\nI\'m now integrating the aim boundary\'s rounded rectangle and scaling with `GameSettings`, ensuring clean separation from the direct arcade steering. I\'ve confirmed that the aiming region is purely visual and non-influential to steering, using `aimBoundaryScale`. Configuration updates are complete, which I am testing with the `FlightController`, `FlightHUD`, and `SettingsPanel`. I will also check the `game.ts` for backward compatibility.\n\n\n**Updating Configuration Settings**\n\nI\'m now implementing the configuration changes, carefully updating `SettingsPanel.tsx` and `FlightHUD.tsx`. I\'ve renamed the settings panel section to `ARCADE FLIGHT CONTROLS`, removing virtual joystick controls and deadzone visualizations, and re-importing the aim boundary. The visual aim boundary scale now has no side effects, and the central reticle is centered, which is exactly as I wanted. All remaining elements are untouched.\n\n\n**Verifying Mouse Input**\n\nI\'m now confirming the clean separation of `InputManager.ts`, as pointer lock remains perfect. The manager is already ideal, focusing purely on `event.movementX`/`Y`, zeroing `mouseDelta` after consumption. This validates our design without modifying the input system, so I\'m moving toward creating `/src/engine/AimBoundary.ts` and configuring the steering parameters.\n\n\n\n"
