  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Refining Arcade Steering**\n\nI\'ve just surgically replaced the virtual-joystick steering block in `/src/engine/FlightController.ts` with a much cleaner implementation: direct relative arcade mouse steering. This preserves essential elements like roll, boost, thrust, lateral grip, and quaternion, ensuring a smoother transition.\n\n\n**Improving Flight Dynamics**\n\nI\'m now focused on refining the flight dynamics. I just corrected the sign-inverted lag offset in `/src/engine/CameraController.ts` to sharpen the chase follow. The `FlightHUD.tsx` adjustments are complete, streamlining the `AimBoundary` usage and removing the deadzone. Also, I cleaned up the `SettingsPanel.tsx`, removing obsolete controls and organizing the `ARCADE FLIGHT CONTROLS` settings. Now I\'m carefully reviewing the yaw and pitch calculations in `FlightController.ts` to ensure everything aligns perfectly with the arcade steering model.\n\n\n**Calculating Mouse Steering Rates**\n\nI\'ve just implemented direct calculation of mouse steering rates, incorporating sensitivity settings for yaw and pitch. The new system calculates angular rates directly from mouse deltas, ensuring precise and responsive control. I\'ve also incorporated a y-axis inversion setting and clamped values.\n\n\n**Refining Angular Control**\n\nI\'m now integrating keyboard yaw and pitch input alongside mouse steering. I\'ve added code to directly calculate `targetYawRate` and `targetPitchRate` using keyboard inputs and the profile\'s yaw and pitch speed settings. Next, I\'m working on smoothing the transition between inputs. I\'m using frame-rate-independent exponential smoothing, which is clamping angular speeds and ensuring zero-drift stops when the inputs cease.\n\n\n**Mapping Reticle Movements**\n\nI\'m now focusing on mapping reticle movement. I\'ve been checking the sign conventions to ensure the reticle leads appropriately with yaw and pitch changes. My goal is to map angular velocities to reticle position using clamped normalized values. Now I\'m carefully constructing the `reticleX` and `reticleY` calculations based on those conventions.\n\n\n**Verifying Reticle Placement**\n\nI\'ve just finished verifying the reticle placement logic to ensure it behaves as expected. The logic confirms that the reticle reacts correctly to mouse movements and is properly bounded within the designated rounded rectangle. With mouse right, I\'m confirming `yawVelocity < 0` results in `reticleX > 0`. Similarly with mouse up, I\'m checking that `pitchVelocity > 0` results in `reticleY < 0`. When the mouse stops, the reticle centers perfectly at `(0.5, 0.5)`. Finally, I\'m examining the `steeringZone` calculations, specifically checking the deadzone and max rate conditions.\n\n\n"
